WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.700
Hi, I'm Eric Minick with IBM Cloud.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.390
I want to tell you a little bit about continuous integration today.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.160
This is a development practice

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.480
that just about everybody thinks they're doing,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.460
but it's widely misunderstood.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.980
So, I want to start by talking about the bad old days before we had it,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.660
what problem we're trying to solve,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.680
and what this does for us and what it's really about.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:28.760
So, let's start with our old school approach

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.040
where we've got a couple of developers,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.280
they're probably on a team of 30 or 50,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:40.480
we got Alice and Bob and they're working on their own features,

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.840
and they're going to keep separate on their own features for a long time.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.620
They're writing some bits of new code here.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.340
Maybe they're deleting a line of code here or there, what have you.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:02.820
And their plan is that they're going to get their features to work against the code base,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.900
then in a couple of weeks, or months, later

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.460
they're going to integrate their work together.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.140
They're going to merge their work together.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:11.460
They're going to say:

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.080
"My code, your code, it's got to come together, it's all got to work".

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:17.880
Now, in this world,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:23.480
you could have a situation where Alice &amp; Bob, they changed the same line of code here, right?

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:29.580
And maybe Alice changed this code, but then Bob deleted it.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:36.620
And so, this gets really awkward, really fast when you just try to algorithmically combine it.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:38.820
You get what's known as a merge
conflict.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:44.300
This gets worse because it might be that some code up here,

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.140
interferes with some code down here,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.760
they just don't work together and you've got bugs.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.060
And this will happen here within just one
file,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.080
but in the real world,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.700
it's not just two developers working on the same file

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.220
it's thousands of files, or millions, in an application.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.780
There's tens of developers involved

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:07.220
and they're doing this over weeks and weeks.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.920
And so, reconciling all of these problems, it's big mess.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.280
It's called "merge hell".

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.960
And so, they say, "What do we do to avoid merge hell? This is how we got here".

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.600
Well, we start, and we'll have Alice writing
some code again, right?

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.320
Alice writes her code.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:31.860
Cool. As soon as she has something that works even a little bit, her feature is not done,

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.900
but she's got code that works, it doesn't break things.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.880
She submits that in the source control, Bob pulls down the same code

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.360
before he starts working, right?

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.560
He always is working off the latest.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.980
Alice is always submitting hers in.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.740
Bob makes his change right?

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.000
Maybe he deletes a line of code, great.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.420
Alice comes back, she's working another part of a feature, she's working on another feature,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.580
she grabs Bob's code. Cool.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.440
And now they're working on these things together.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:08.940
And the likelihood that they're both working on the same thing, at the same time,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.860
and that they conflict is pretty small.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:17.000
And if they do hit that, it's all on code
they've worked on in the last day, right?

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.740
they can sit together, they can reconcile
this thing, it's no big deal.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.180
And so, the core principle that we're seeing here,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.000
one of my favorite things in software development, because it is so counterintuitive,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.920
is that if it hurts, do it often and it won't hurt so much.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.520
And that helps us get rid of the big, big bogey here, right?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.200
Which is, we're going to not have merge hell.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.260
We don't like merge hell. Get rid of that.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.480
OK, so that's fix number one.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.720
Now, this does create a new problem though, right?

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:03.340
They have a whole bunch of people constantly checking in code into the code base.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.340
Things are going to break, right?

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.100
It's not going to compile.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:11.080
There's going to be bugs that weren't there yesterday.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:17.200
And you have this kind of, "continuous integration led to continuously broken" problem.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.000
And that's sad.  So, what do we do?

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.500
Well, we put some automation in, right?

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.580
And it's always watching the code.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.140
Keeping an eye on it,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:29.600
and whenever a change happens,

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:38.400
It says, "OK, let me grab that code. I'm going to build it."

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.240
"And then I'm going to test it."

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.620
"Alright, I'm going to run my unit tests".

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.560
So, if there's any change, build it, test it.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.720
If anything in here fails, then we email the team.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.600
We let Alice, Bob, the whole team know, say

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.380
"Hey, there were 2 new changes since last time I did a build,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.240
looks like both Alice and Bob touched this thing.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.180
It's broken now. They need to go fix the code base."

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:11.780
Good. So, now we've got a safety net that enables continuous integration. Good.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:18.380
The other thing that we're getting though, is that we always have this build. Right?

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.980
So, whenever the test team wants to go test it in more depth,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:27.360
to run a heavier level of tests than the unit testing that we're doing here,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.700
they've got a build they know compiles -

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.840
and it's the newest stuff, right?

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:36.500
So, that's our other key benefit coming out of continuous integration

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:41.740
is that we always have a testable build.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:42.940
So, there you go.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:48.300
Continuous integration was addressing the old way of infrequent integration,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.060
saying, "If it hurts, we're going to do it continuously, we're going to do it a lot."

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:56.560
That avoids merge hell, keeps our developers productive,

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:00.200
and it gives us a constantly testable build.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.060
So that's continuous integration in a nutshell.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.360
Thank you.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.540
If you have questions, please drop us a line.

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:13.160
If you want to see more videos like this in the future, be sure to like and subscribe.

